globalThis.COURSE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence","anchor":"academic-integrity-collaboration-and-artificial-intelligence","text":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence You are encouraged to work with classmates, tutors, and me: talking through ideas, definitions, and problem-solving strategies is a valuable part of learning. You must understand every solution you submit and write it based on your own understanding. Please do not copy another person’s solution or write-up. You may use generative artificial intelligence as a study aid, but not to produce work that you submit for credit. For example, it is fine to ask for another explanation of the difference between pointwise and convergence. It is not acceptable to ask an AI system to solve, outline, or check a specific assigned problem. Rephrasing a generated solution in your own words is not acceptable. If you are unsure whether a particular use is allowed, ask me before submitting. When you submit an assignment, please disclose any collaboration or use of external tools, even when it is permitted."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Assignments","anchor":"assignments","text":"Assignments"},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions usually follow each lecture and are due at the start of our next class meeting. They can be accessed through Blackboard > Assignments > Online Questions and the deadlines will appear in your Blackboard Calendar. Online questions give you a quick chance to practice vocabulary and core skills before we build on the material. Most questions allow multiple attempts."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Weekly written homework","anchor":"weekly-written-homework","text":"Weekly written homework I will post written homework here approximately weekly. Submit through Gradescope using the links in Blackboard > Assignments > Homework. You are encouraged to work in groups of up to three. Make one submission per group and list every group member clearly. Start each exercise on a new page. Scan or export the complete assignment as a legible PDF. In Gradescope, match every exercise to the pages containing its solution. Write in complete sentences, state the results you use, and make the logical structure of each proof clear. I will normally post solutions within one week of the submission deadline. Deadlines will appear in your Blackboard Calendar."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Calendar","anchor":"calendar","text":"Calendar I will put all deadlines on your Blackboard Calendar. Please check it regularly!"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Daily lecture log","anchor":"daily-lecture-log","text":"Daily lecture log Week Lec. Date Topics Read 1 1 Mon., Aug. 17 Review: the Riemann integral; theorem+proof: continuous functions are Riemann integrable; review: continuous functions, uniformly continuous functions, modulus of continuity; theorem (statement only): a function is Riemann integrable if and only if the set of its discontinuities has Lebesgue measure zero; anticipation of definition - Lebesgue measure zero: countable covers by intervals with small total lenght; the norm L1 on continuous functions; review: definition of norm; remark: L1 is not a norm on Riemann Integrable functions because separation axiom fails; Proposition (no proof yet): continuous functions with L1 norm are not a complete metric space. Lecture notes LN: Review of Riemann integration. 2 Wed., Aug. 19 Proposition + proof: continuous functions with L1 norm are not a complete metric space. Review: existence and uniqueness of completion and extension of uniformly continuous maps; the Lebesgue integral as the completion of and the Riemann integrals with respect to . The fat rationals; sketch why this shows that Riemann integrable functions with respect to are incomplete. Tentative !TODO! 3 Fri., Aug. 21 Finite, countable, and uncountable sets; countable unions and products. Extended nonnegative reals; arbitrary sums of non-negative quantities. Monotone convergence and Tonelli for nonnegative series; continuity of arbitrary sums; Tentative 2 4 Mon., Aug. 24 Cost functionals; finite additivity, countable additivity, and countable subadditivity; outer measures generated by coverings. Tentative Axler, pp. 14-24. 5 Wed., Aug. 26 Cost functionals; finite additivity, countable additivity, and countable subadditivity; outer measures generated by coverings. Tentative Axler, pp. 14-24. 6 Fri., Aug. 28 examples of generated outer measures. Monotonicity and countable subadditivity; when a generated outer measure extends its cost functional; comparison principles. Tentative 3 7 Mon., Aug. 31 Rectangles and volume; Lebesgue outer measure in dimensions one and two; the outer measure of a rectangle. Tentative 8 Wed., Sep. 2 ? Tentative 9 Fri., Sep. 4 Computing generated outer measures; finite additivity and countable superadditivity on disjoint families. Tentative 4 — Mon., Sep. 7 Labor Day holiday; no class. Tentative 10 Wed., Sep. 9 Translation invariance of Lebesgue outer measure; failure of countable additivity on all subsets; the Vitali construction. Tentative Axler, Propositions 2.18 and 2.22. 11 Fri., Sep. 11 Rings, algebras, and -algebras; generated and Borel -algebras; additive and countably additive set functions. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 1.1-1.3. 5 12 Mon., Sep. 14 Measures as countably additive functions; continuity from below and from above; set limsup and liminf. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 1.3. 13 Wed., Sep. 16 Carathéodory’s criterion; measurable sets for an outer measure; proof that they form an algebra. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 1.5.2. 14 Fri., Sep. 18 The Cantor set; -algebras generated by rectangles and singletons; dyadic decompositions and maximal elements. Tentative 6 15 Mon., Sep. 21 Closure under countable unions; restricting an outer measure to its measurable sets; Lebesgue and Borel measurable sets. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 1.5.2. 16 Wed., Sep. 23 Measurable and Borel functions; pullbacks of -algebras; checking measurability on generators; continuous functions are Borel measurable. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 2.1-2.2. 17 Fri., Sep. 25 Set-theoretic descriptions of level sets; closure under algebraic operations; measurable limits through countable set operations. Tentative 7 18 Mon., Sep. 28 Pointwise limits of measurable functions; simple functions; monotone approximation of nonnegative measurable functions. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 2.2-2.3, especially Proposition 2.4. 19 Wed., Sep. 30 Superlevel-set distribution functions; right continuity and left limits; the Archimedean integral and layer-cake definition. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 2.4.2-2.4.3. 20 Fri., Oct. 2 Integrals of indicator and simple functions; equivalent representations; explicit superlevel-set computations. Tentative 8 21 Mon., Oct. 5 Definition and basic properties; monotonicity, Markov’s inequality, monotone convergence, and linearity. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 2.4.4. 22 Wed., Oct. 7 Positive and negative parts; integrability; linearity and the triangle inequality; equality almost everywhere. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 2.5. 23 Fri., Oct. 9 Fatou’s lemma; deduction of the dominated convergence theorem; measures defined by integrable densities. Tentative 9 24 Mon., Oct. 12 Uniform and equi-uniform integrability; Egorov’s theorem; Vitali convergence and a second route to dominated convergence. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 2.6. 25 Wed., Oct. 14 Choosing among monotone convergence, Fatou, dominated convergence, Egorov, and uniform integrability; hypothesis-checking and counterexamples. Tentative 26 Fri., Oct. 16 Agreement on continuous functions; upper and lower semicontinuous envelopes; the Lebesgue criterion for Riemann integrability. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 2.7. 10 — Mon., Oct. 19 Fall Break; no class. Tentative 27 Wed., Oct. 21 Functions defined almost everywhere; equivalence classes; the norm and the completeness problem. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 3.1 and 3.3. 28 Fri., Oct. 23 Improper versus Lebesgue integrals; parameter-dependent limits; applications and failure modes of domination. Tentative 11 29 Mon., Oct. 26 Cauchy sequences, rapidly convergent subsequences, almost-everywhere convergence, and completeness of . Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 3.3. 30 Wed., Oct. 28 Definitions and examples; sequence spaces; essential supremum; Young’s inequality and Hölder’s inequality. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 3.1, 3.2.1, and 3.4. 31 Fri., Oct. 30 Hölder with several factors; sharp exponent tests using characteristic functions; finite-measure inclusions. Tentative 12 32 Mon., Nov. 2 Minkowski’s inequality; norm structure; completeness for . Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Sections 3.2-3.3. 33 Wed., Nov. 4 Almost-everywhere, in-measure, and convergence; counterexamples; rapidly convergent subsequences. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 3.3. 34 Fri., Nov. 6 Constructing separating examples; using domination in ; norm continuity for nonlinear expressions. Tentative 13 35 Mon., Nov. 9 -finite measure spaces; density of simple integrable functions in ; the exceptional behavior of . Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 3.5, especially Proposition 3.15. 36 Wed., Nov. 11 Approximation in Borel measure spaces; density of compactly supported continuous functions in Euclidean spaces. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Section 3.5, especially Proposition 3.16. 37 Fri., Nov. 13 Continuity of translations and dilations in ; density arguments; why analogous statements can fail. Tentative 14 38 Mon., Nov. 16 Testing the norm against ; endpoint cases; log-convexity, interpolation, and decompositions between spaces. Tentative 39 Wed., Nov. 18 Pointwise definition by Hölder; boundedness and continuity; approximation by compactly supported continuous functions. Tentative 40 Fri., Nov. 20 Selecting among Hölder, Minkowski, density, and convergence arguments; counterexamples at endpoints. Tentative 15 41 Mon., Nov. 23 Measurable rectangles; product -algebras; sections of sets and functions; construction and uniqueness issues for product measures. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Chapter 7. — Wed., Nov. 25 Thanksgiving Break; no class. Tentative — Fri., Nov. 27 Thanksgiving holiday; no class. Tentative 16 42 Mon., Nov. 30 Iterated integrals of nonnegative measurable functions; measurability of sections; examples with infinite integrals. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Chapter 7. 43 Wed., Dec. 2 Integrable functions on product spaces; changing the order of integration; why absolute integrability matters; connections among the course’s main constructions and theorems. Tentative Ambrosio-Da Prato-Mennucci, Chapter 7. Tentative: Future lecture topics and their placement may change as the course develops."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Exams","anchor":"exams","text":"Exams Date Event Week of Wednesday, October 12 - Oct 18 , 2026, Tentative Written midterm (two hours, outside the regular class period) Wednesday, December 9, 2026, 10:15 a.m.-12:15 p.m. Final-exam period"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Key university dates","anchor":"key-university-dates","text":"Key university dates For official University dates, see the Fall 2026 academic calendar and the Registrar’s final-exam schedule. Date Event Monday, August 17 First day of classes Friday, August 21 Last day to add this course through the standard add period Friday, August 28 Last day to drop without a W Thursday, December 3 Last day of classes and last day to withdraw from all classes"},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"REAL VARIABLES I MATH55003-01 – Fall 2026","heading":"REAL VARIABLES I MATH55003-01 – Fall 2026","anchor":"real-variables-i-math55003-01-fall-2026","text":"REAL VARIABLES I MATH55003-01 – Fall 2026 Semester: Fall 2026 Instructor: Gennady Uraltsev Meetings: Mo/We/Fr 9:40 - 10:30, CHPN 421 Welcome to Real Variables I, a graduate introduction to measure theory and Lebesgue integration. We will build measure and integration from first principles, study the convergence theorems that make the Lebesgue integral effective, and develop the structure of the spaces ."},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"REAL VARIABLES I MATH55003-01 – Fall 2026","heading":"Start here","anchor":"start-here","text":"Start here Syllabus: how the course works, grading, materials, and what to expect. Course calendar: class dates, current topics, holidays, and exams. Assignments: written homework, online questions, and submission policies. Resources: textbooks, office-hour guidance, and student-support links. Lecture notes: notes organized by course unit and lecture. Please check the course calendar for holidays, University deadlines, and topic updates."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Lecture Notes","anchor":"lecture-notes","text":"Lecture Notes Lecture notes will be added here as the semester progresses. Until a note is published, use the reading listed in the daily lecture log."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Planned unit map","anchor":"planned-unit-map","text":"Planned unit map Tentative Unit Topics I Motivation, null sets, countability, and outer measure II -algebras, measures, and Carathéodory measurability III Measurable functions and the Lebesgue integral IV Integral limit theorems and uniform integrability V spaces, inequalities, completeness, convergence, and density VI Product measures, Tonelli’s theorem, and Fubini’s theorem"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"Digression on uniform continuity and modulus of continuity","anchor":"digression-on-uniform-continuity-and-modulus-of-continuity","text":"Digression on uniform continuity and modulus of continuity The proof above essentially relies on the following reasoning. Suppose every subinterval of a partition has length at most . If the values of differ by at most whenever their inputs are within of one another, then every oscillation in (1) is at most , and therefore The point is that one value of must work everywhere on , not merely near one point at a time. Definition (Uniform continuity) Let and let . The function is uniformly continuous on if, for every , there exists such that for all , The number may depend on , but it must work for every pair . For continuity at a point , the choice of may depend on . A function can therefore be continuous at every point while requiring smaller and smaller scales as the point moves through the domain. Uniform continuity rules this out by requiring one common scale. Theorem (Review: Heine–Cantor theorem) Let be compact. Every continuous function is uniformly continuous on . In particular, every continuous function on a closed bounded interval is uniformly continuous. The proof is a prototypical compactness argument, which we omit as it is beyond the scope of this exposition, but we invite the reader to refresh their memory. Generally, continuous functions are not uniformly continuous. Problem Let . Show that is continuous on but it is uniformly continuous there only if . The modulus of continuity encodes the relationship of uniform continuity by rercoding how much a function can change at each spatial scale. Definition (Modulus of continuity) Let be an interval and let . A modulus of continuity for is a nondecreasing function such that and The natural choice is the worst oscillation over all pairs separated by at most : (3) We suggest the reader to recall and know how to prove the following fact. Proposition (Review: Uniform continuity is equivalent to having a modulus) Let be an interval or a finite union of disjoint intervals and let . Then is uniformly continuous on if and only if it has a modulus of continuity. When is uniformly continuous, the function in (3) is finite for every and is a modulus of continuity. We can now complete the argument that motivated uniform continuity."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"Review of Riemann Integration","anchor":"review-of-riemann-integration","text":"Review of Riemann Integration We review breifly review the construction and properties of the Riemann integral and identify its shortcomings that suggest constructing a new integration theory: the Lebesgue integral First, we show that continuous are a natural class on which the Riemann integral is defined. However some discontinuities can be tolerated - we anticipate a result we prove later in the course that provides a complete characterization of Riemann integrable functions. However, this result is formulated in terms of quantities appearing in Lebesgue integration theory. Second, we use the integral to measure the distance between functions and thereby introduce the norm . We will show that continuous functions with the distance induced by the norm is not a complete metric space, thereby having the same issues for the purpose of analysis as the rationals have with respect to the reals. Abstract metric space theory tells us that there exists a unique completion of the space of continuous functions, to which the Riemann integral can be extended to. This happens to be exactly the Lebesgue integral. This description is completely opaque, but it does provide an important goal: create an integration theory such that the associated spaces of integrable functions are complete."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"Riemann integrability","anchor":"riemann-integrability","text":"Riemann integrability A function is Riemann integrable if lower and upper Riemann sum approximations can be made arbitrarily close. Definition (Partition of an interval) Let with . A partition of is a finite ordered tuple such that The mesh of the partition is The number of intervals in the partition is Warning The term partition is heavily overloaded in mathematics. Often it stands for an arbitrary representation of a set as a disjoint union. Here, we partition an interval into finitely many ordered intervals. Definition (Upper and lower Riemann sums) The upper Riemann sum and lower Riemann sum of with respect to are The lower sum never exceeds the upper sum. Refining a partition can only raise the lower sum and lower the upper sum. Definition (Riemann integrability) A bounded function is Riemann integrable if where the supremum and infimum are taken over all finite partitions of . The common value is denoted by Therefore, to show that a function is Riemann integrable it is sufficient to show that by choosing an appropriate partition , the quantity can be made arbitrarily small. This is also necessary because for any given if for two different partitions and then is taken to be a common refinement of the partitions . According to this reduction, we must show that (1) can be made arbitrarily small, where the oscillation of over an interval is defined as (2) We are ready to prove the following theorem. Theorem (Continuous functions are Riemann integrable) Every continuous function is Riemann integrable. Proof (Proof that continuity implies Riemann integrability) The claim follows by bounding (1). Since is continuous and is compact, it admits a modulus of continuity i.e. a function such that as for any . We have the bound where we used that It is therefore sufficient to choose a partition such that and, therefore, is as small as desired."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"Riemann integrability of functions with discontinuities.","anchor":"riemann-integrability-of-functions-with-discontinuities","text":"Riemann integrability of functions with discontinuities. Continuity controlled the oscillation on every subinterval. If a bounded function has finitely many discontinuities, that control fails near those points. The proof can still succeed by treating two regions differently. Away from the discontinuities, continuity makes the oscillation small. Near the discontinuities, one uses boundedness of and smallness of the intervals in containing the discontinuities. Proposition (Bounded functions with finitely many discontinuities are Riemann integrable) Let be bounded. If has only finitely many discontinuities, then is Riemann integrable. We provide a proof for completeness, but it is generally of little interest for this course and the reader is invited to skip it. Proof Let be the set of discontinuities. By assumption . Fix sufficiently small and note that is uniformly continuous on the the finite union of intervals with being the exceptional set at scale . Let be the modulus of continuity of on . Given any partition , let , that is the collection of indexes such that the interval intersects the exceptional set . Therefore, for any partition , For the first summand we have that because all intervals in play are in . For the second summand we use that but that since the intervals touch the exceptional set , consisting of -neighborhoods of the points , the intervals themselves have lenght at most . Therefore and so Combining the two estimates we obtain that By choosing with as small as required, we obtain that Now, since was arbitary we obtain that This proof shows that finiteness is not the essential issue. What mattered was the ability to cover all discontinuities by intervals whose total length is as small as desired. That observation leads to the exact characterization that will be proved later in the course. Definition (Lebesgue null set) A set has Lebesgue measure zero, or is a Lebesgue null set, if for every there exists a sequence of open intervals such that and A null set need not be finite. The definition says that, from the point of view of total interval length, the set can be confined to a region of arbitrarily small “length”. Theorem (Preview: Lebesgue’s criterion for Riemann integrability) Let be bounded, and let Then is Riemann integrable if and only if has Lebesgue measure zero. No proof is given here. The theorem states the destination of the first part of the course: Riemann integrability is governed not by the number of discontinuities, but by whether their locations can be covered by intervals of arbitrarily small total length. This last quantity is a prototypical construction in Lebesgue measure theory."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"The abstract repair: completion","anchor":"the-abstract-repair-completion","text":"The abstract repair: completion The examples tell us what is wrong with the starting spaces: the integral distance produces Cauchy sequences whose limits are absent. We can use abstract metric completion to “fill in the gaps” and add one new element for each missing limiting behavior. Definition (Completion of a metric space) A completion of a metric space is a complete metric space together with an isometric embedding such that is dense in . Theorem (Review: Existence and uniqueness of metric completion) Every metric space has a completion. Any two completions are isometric by an isometry that preserves the embedded copy of the original space. In the standard construction, an element of is an equivalence class of Cauchy sequence in : two Cauchy sequences represent the same element when the distance between their corresponding terms tends to zero. Applied to with the integral norm, this construction formally supplies all missing limits. We can extend the integral intself to the completion using the existence of continuous extensions. Proposition (Review: Uniformly continuous maps extend to completions) Let be a metric space, let be a completion of , and let be complete. Every uniformly continuous map has a unique continuous extension Define For , Thus the integral is -Lipschitz: functions that are close in integral norm have close integrals. In particular, if is Cauchy in the integral norm, then is Cauchy in and therefore converges. Because is Lipschitz and is complete, extends uniquely to the completion of in the integral norm. Concretely, if an element of the completion is represented by a Cauchy sequence , its integral is defined by The Lipschitz estimate guarantees both that this limit exists and that equivalent Cauchy sequences give the same value. This construction accomplishes two formal goals: it creates a complete space and extends the integral to every new element. It does not yet give a satisfactory concrete description of those elements. An equivalence class of Cauchy sequences of continuous functions is an abstract object; from that description alone, it is not even clear whether the new element is representable as a “function”. The measure-theoretic construction provides a concrete model for this abstract completion. After Lebesgue measure and integration have been developed, the completion above can be identified with the Lebesgue space"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration","pageTitle":"Review of Riemann Integration","heading":"The norm and incompleteness","anchor":"the-l-1-norm-and-incompleteness","text":"The norm and incompleteness On , define that measures the “average” (if you devide by ) or integral magnitude of a function . We will see that is a norm and therefore induces the distance The quantity measures the average (or integral) discrepancy between and . A large pointwise error can have small integral cost when it is confined to a short interval - a feature that may be desired in specific approximation circumstances. Before testing limits, we verify that is a norm on continuous functions. Nonnegativity, homogeneity, and the triangle inequality follow from the corresponding properties of the absolute value. The remaining issue is whether zero integral forces a continuous function to vanish everywhere. Lemma (Zero integral forces a continuous function to vanish) Let . If then on . Proof Suppose that for some . By continuity at , there exists such that for every This set contains an interval of positive length. Integrating the positive lower bound over that interval gives contrary to the hypothesis. Hence vanishes everywhere. We can now ask whether every sequence that should have a limit in this distance actually has one in . Definition (Complete metric space) A sequence in a metric space is Cauchy if, for every , there exists such that A metric space is complete if every Cauchy sequence converges (i.e. has a limit). However, is not complete with respect to the distance. Proposition (Continuous functions are not complete in the integral norm) The normed space is not complete. Proof For , define Each is continuous. Define the Riemann-integrable step function If it holds that Indeed, Hence is Cauchy in . Suppose that in this norm for some . One can guess what the missing limit function should be: it is the step function . The sequence fails to converge inside because continuity is not preserved by convergence in . The step function is Riemann integrable, so one might try to repair the problem by replacing continuous functions with all Riemann-integrable functions. While a much more subtle argument, we will use Lebesgue theory later in the course to see that Riemann-integrable functions are not complete with respect to the distance. Warning Let be the vector space of Riemann-integrable functions on , and define For we have but Thus is not a norm on pointwise-defined Riemann-integrable functions. To obtain a norm, functions at zero distance must be identified. Even if we carefully avoid the problem above, some Cauchy of Riemann-integrable functions still failt to converge to a Riemann-integrable limit function. Proposition (Riemann-integrable functions are incomplete with respect to L^1.) There exists a sequence of continuous functions on that is Cauchy with respect to but there does not exist a Riemann-integrable function on such that We do not present the proof here. It will be simple once we have developped Lebesgue integration theory."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/index","pageTitle":"I. Introduction to measures","heading":"I. Introduction to measures","anchor":"i-introduction-to-measures","text":"I. Introduction to measures"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Access and well-being","anchor":"access-and-well-being","text":"Access and well-being Center for Educational Access Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares University emergency information RazALERT"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Additional references","anchor":"additional-references","text":"Additional references Elias M. Stein and Rami Shakarchi, Real Analysis: Measure Theory, Integration, and Hilbert Spaces, Princeton Lectures in Analysis III, Princeton University Press, 2005. Book record. Sheldon Axler, Measure, Integration & Real Analysis, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 282, Springer, 2020. The author provides a legal, free electronic edition. Open-access book. Gerald B. Folland, Real Analysis: Modern Techniques and Their Applications, 2nd ed., Wiley, 1999. This is a more advanced reference with extensive coverage beyond the course. Publisher page."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Main textbook","anchor":"main-textbook","text":"Main textbook Luigi Ambrosio, Giuseppe Da Prato, and Andrea Mennucci, Introduction to Measure Theory and Integration, Publications of the Scuola Normale Superiore 10, Edizioni della Normale, 2011. This is our principal reference. Publisher page and electronic access."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Office hours","anchor":"office-hours","text":"Office hours Office hours are part of the course. Bring questions about definitions, proofs, examples, reading, or homework - even when your question is only “where should I start?” Discussing homework in office hours is encouraged. If you give a clear, complete solution of a designated homework exercise, I may propose to you a grade for that exercise during the meeting. If you are satisfied with the grade, you will not need to submit a written solution for that exercise."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Resources","anchor":"resources","text":"Resources For every topic we cover, I will provide either lecture notes or a precise reading reference. The books below offer complementary treatments; you do not need to purchase them."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Academic integrity","anchor":"academic-integrity","text":"Academic integrity We all share responsibility for creating a course environment built on honest work and learning. Please follow the University of Arkansas Academic Integrity Policy; suspected violations are handled through the University process. Specific expectations for collaboration and artificial intelligence appear on the assignments page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Accessibility and accommodations","anchor":"accessibility-and-accommodations","text":"Accessibility and accommodations If you have approved accommodations, please request your accommodation letter and contact me early so we can make arrangements in time. If you think you may need accommodations but have not yet set them up, the Center for Educational Access can help. Temporary conditions can affect access too, so please reach out promptly when they arise."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Assessment","anchor":"assessment","text":"Assessment Grade weights Component Weight Written hand-in homework 30% Online questions 10% Midterm 15% Final 15% Oral exam 1 15% Oral exam 2 15% Total 100% Grade weights breakdownWritten hand-in homework 30%; Online questions 10%; Midterm 15%; Final 15%; Oral exam 1 15%; Oral exam 2 15%.Written hand-in homework: 30%Online questions: 10%Midterm: 15%Final: 15%Oral exam 1: 15%Oral exam 2: 15%Written hand-in homework 30%Online questions 10%Midterm 15%Final 15%Oral exam 1 15%Oral exam 2 15%Dropped assignments: The lowest 2 written-homework scores are dropped. The lowest 4 online-question scores are dropped. One additional online-question score is dropped if you complete all check-in work."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Attendance and participation","anchor":"attendance-and-participation","text":"Attendance and participation Attendance is strongly encouraged but not graded, and there is no absence penalty. If you need to miss class, use the posted notes, readings, online questions, homework, and other resources to keep up independently. Please keep track of course material, announcements, deadlines, and assessments. If your online work, homework, or exams suggest that your current study routine is not serving you, I may reach out with ideas such as regular attendance or office hours. Please plan to take scheduled exams on time. Make-up exams are available only for excused conflicts or emergencies."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Communication, records, and course changes","anchor":"communication-records-and-course-changes","text":"Communication, records, and course changes Email is the best way to reach me with an individual question. I will post course-wide announcements, grades, and updates in Blackboard, so please check your University email and Blackboard regularly. If you notice a possible grading or recording error, please let me know within one week of the posted score. I may revise the syllabus when needed. If a change materially affects grading, exams, deadlines, or course delivery, I will announce it in writing. The current version of this website is our course reference."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course organization","anchor":"course-organization","text":"Course organization In class, I will introduce new topics, develop proofs, and work through examples and applications with you. I will assign weekly written homework. Fridays will usually emphasize problem solving, focused especially on appropriately marked problems from the homework. If you give a substantially correct presentation of a problem designated for discussion, I may propose a grade immediately; if you are happy with that grade, you will not need to include that problem in the written submission. After most class meetings, online questions provide a quick review of basic skills. They are graded automatically, and most allow multiple attempts. We will have two written exams: a two-hour, self-proctored midterm and a two-hour final. We will also have two oral exams. Before each exam, I will publish a list of eligible topics. At the exam I will give you a choice between two topics and you will have 40 minutes to prepare, followed by about 30 minutes for your presentation and a mathematical discussion."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course overview","anchor":"course-overview","text":"Course overview University of Arkansas mathematics course listings Prerequisite: MATH 45203 or MATH 52203; graduate standing in mathematics or statistics; or departmental consent. This course develops the foundations of Lebesgue measure and integration and the spaces that underpin modern analysis, partial differential equations, probability, and harmonic analysis. We begin by identifying limitations of Riemann integration, then we construct measures - ways of describing how large a set is. We then develop measurable functions and use the notion of measure to construct the Lebesgue integral, describing how large functions are. Next, we prove the major limit theorems for the Lebesgue integral, then and study normed spaces of integrable functions. The final part of the course treats product measures and the Tonelli-Fubini theorems. Course contents Unit Main capability From Riemann integration to measure Explain why a more flexible integral is needed; work with countability, null sets, outer measures, and the extended real line. -algebras and measures Construct and analyze measurable spaces, Borel sets, measures, and Carathéodory-measurable sets; prove continuity properties of measures. Measurable functions and Lebesgue integration Verify measurability, approximate nonnegative functions by simple functions, define the integral, and use monotone convergence, Fatou’s lemma, and dominated convergence. spaces Work with almost-everywhere equivalence, Hölder and Minkowski inequalities, completeness, modes of convergence, density, and basic duality estimates. Product measures and iterated integration Set up product measure spaces and apply Tonelli’s and Fubini’s theorems with the correct hypotheses."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Inclement weather and emergencies","anchor":"inclement-weather-and-emergencies","text":"Inclement weather and emergencies We will follow University closure and inclement-weather decisions. If a closure prevents an exam, I will announce a new date through Blackboard and the course website. Please do not travel when conditions are unsafe. University emergency information RazALERT emergency notifications Academic Policy 1858.10: inclement weather"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Learning outcomes","anchor":"learning-outcomes","text":"Learning outcomes By the end of the course, you should be able to: State and use the definitions of outer measure, -algebra, measure, measurable function, almost-everywhere equality, and the Lebesgue integral. Construct Lebesgue measure through outer measure and Carathéodory’s criterion, and distinguish measurable from nonmeasurable sets. Prove measurability and integrability claims using approximation by simple functions and superlevel-set methods. Select and apply monotone convergence, Fatou’s lemma, dominated convergence, Egorov’s theorem, and uniform-integrability arguments. Compare Riemann and Lebesgue integration. Analyze spaces using Hölder’s and Minkowski’s inequalities, completeness, density, convergence modes, and norm-testing arguments. Use product measures and Tonelli-Fubini to justify iterated integration and interchange the order of integration. Communicate graduate-level analysis in complete written proofs and in a prepared oral presentation, including motivation, examples, and counterexamples."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Letter grades","anchor":"letter-grades","text":"Letter grades The following scale is the tentative baseline. If it changes, I will announce the revision in writing; a change will not make an already earned letter grade lower. Course percentage Letter grade 88-100 A 76-87.99 B 64-75.99 C 51-63.99 D Below 51 F"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Materials","anchor":"materials","text":"Materials My lecture notes are our main guide through the course. They may also link to explanatory videos or other freely available sources. The principal reference is Luigi Ambrosio, Giuseppe Da Prato, and Andrea Mennucci, Introduction to Measure Theory and Integration, Publications of the Scuola Normale Superiore 10, Edizioni della Normale, 2011, doi:10.1007/978-88-7642-386-4. Additional references are listed on the resources page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions normally follow a lecture and are due by the start of our next class meeting. They focus on fluency with key vocabulary, examples, and basic deductions."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Oral exams","anchor":"oral-exams","text":"Oral exams For each oral exam, I will announce a topic list in advance. At the appointment you will receive two topics, choose one, and have 40 minutes to organize a board presentation. The presentation and follow-up discussion will last about 30 minutes. You should explain the motivation and precise statement, present the central proof, and discuss relevant examples, counterexamples, and limitations. Assessment emphasizes mathematical correctness, command of the ideas, and your ability to respond thoughtfully to questions."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Other information","anchor":"other-information","text":"Other information"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts","anchor":"religious-observances-university-activities-and-other-excusable-conflicts","text":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts Please email me as early as possible about religious observances, required University activities, military service, jury duty, documented illness, a family crisis, or another excusable conflict. For an exam conflict you can anticipate, at least one week’s notice is especially helpful. We will follow applicable University policies and deadlines. If you have three or more final exams on one day, you may be able to reschedule one under Academic Policy 1500.20. If it involves this course, please make your request before the Registrar’s withdrawal deadline."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Student support","anchor":"student-support","text":"Student support Mathematics Resource and Teaching Center Student Success Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares Additional links are collected on the resources page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Syllabus","anchor":"syllabus","text":"Syllabus Item Information Course REAL VARIABLES I MATH55003-01 Semester Fall 2026 Instructor Gennady Uraltsev Email uraltsev@uark.edu Office SCEN 421 Class meetings Mo/We/Fr 9:40 - 10:30, CHPN 421 Office hours TBA, SCEN 421"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Weekly hand-in homework","anchor":"weekly-hand-in-homework","text":"Weekly hand-in homework Each homework is graded on a four-point scale. After feedback is returned, you have two days to resubmit; if the revision improves the solution, the higher score is recorded. If you need more time, talk to me before the deadline so that we can work out an extension. We will handle unforeseen emergencies case by case."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Written exams","anchor":"written-exams","text":"Written exams There are two two-hour written exams: one midterm and one final. Each covers roughly half of the course topics. The midterm will be completed outside normal class hours; the final follows the Registrar’s schedule."}];
